I'll solve this directly. Note the problem quantifies over $a,b,c,d$ but the expression uses only $a,b,c$; since $d$ is free, it suffices to prove the statement for all $a,b,c>0$.

---

## Proof

Set $x = \tfrac{b}{a},\; y = \tfrac{c}{b},\; z = \tfrac{a}{c}$. Then $x,y,z > 0$ and $xyz = 1$, and

$$
\frac{4a+b}{a+4b}=\frac{4+x}{1+4x},\qquad
\frac{4b+c}{b+4c}=\frac{4+y}{1+4y},\qquad
\frac{4c+a}{c+4a}=\frac{4+z}{1+4z}.
$$

Define $f(t)=\dfrac{4+t}{1+4t}$. We prove

$$
3 \;\le\; f(x)+f(y)+f(z) \;<\; \frac{33}{4}, \qquad xyz=1,\; x,y,z>0.
$$

### Lower bound: $f(x)+f(y)+f(z)\ge 3$

Since $f(t)-1 = \dfrac{4+t-(1+4t)}{1+4t}=\dfrac{3(1-t)}{1+4t}$, the claim is equivalent to

$$
\sum_{\text{cyc}}\frac{1-x}{1+4x}\ge 0.
$$

All denominators are positive, so multiplying by $(1+4x)(1+4y)(1+4z)$ and using $xyz=1$, the numerator equals (expanding each $(1-x)(1+4y)(1+4z)$ cyclically and collecting terms)

$$
-45+7(x+y+z)+8(xy+yz+xz).
$$

Thus it suffices to show $7s+8q\ge 45$ where $s=x+y+z$ and $q=xy+yz+xz$. By AM–GM and $xyz=1$,

$$
s\ge 3\sqrt[3]{xyz}=3,\qquad q\ge 3\sqrt[3]{(xyz)^2}=3,
$$

hence $7s+8q\ge 7\cdot 3+8\cdot 3=45$. Equality holds iff $x=y=z=1$, i.e. $a=b=c$. So $f(x)+f(y)+f(z)\ge 3$, with equality attainable.

### Upper bound: $f(x)+f(y)+f(z)<\dfrac{33}{4}$

Rewrite

$$
f(t)=\frac{4+t}{1+4t}=\frac14+\frac{15}{4(1+4t)},
$$

so

$$
f(x)+f(y)+f(z)=\frac34+\frac{15}{4}\sum_{\text{cyc}}\frac{1}{1+4x}.
$$

It is enough to prove $\displaystyle\sum_{\text{cyc}}\frac{1}{1+4x}<2$.

Since every denominator is positive, this is equivalent (after clearing $(1+4x)(1+4y)(1+4z)>0$) to

$$
(1+4y)(1+4z)+(1+4x)(1+4z)+(1+4x)(1+4y)\;<\;2(1+4x)(1+4y)(1+4z).
$$

The left side expands to $3+8s+16q$. The right side, using $xyz=1$, expands to

$$
2\bigl(1+4s+16q+64xyz\bigr)=2(65+4s+16q)=130+8s+32q.
$$

The difference (RHS $-$ LHS) is

$$
(130+8s+32q)-(3+8s+16q)=127+16q>0,
$$

since $q=xy+yz+xz>0$. Therefore $\displaystyle\sum_{\text{cyc}}\frac{1}{1+4x}<2$ strictly, and consequently

$$
f(x)+f(y)+f(z)<\frac34+\frac{15}{4}\cdot 2=\frac34+\frac{30}{4}=\frac{33}{4}.
$$

### Sharpness of the upper bound

The constant $\frac{33}{4}$ cannot be decreased: take $x=y=t,\; z=\tfrac{1}{t^2}$ with $t\to 0^+$. Then $f(t)\to 4$ and $f(1/t^2)\to \tfrac14$, so

$$
f(x)+f(y)+f(z)\to 4+4+\tfrac14=\frac{33}{4},
$$

yet the value is never attained. Hence the strict upper bound is best possible.

### Conclusion

For all $a,b,c>0$ (and trivially for all $d>0$, which does not appear),

$$
3\;\le\;\frac{4a+b}{a+4b}+\frac{4b+c}{b+4c}+\frac{4c+a}{c+4a}\;<\;\frac{33}{4},
$$

with equality on the left iff $a=b=c$, and the right inequality strict (and sharp). The statement is therefore **true**.

